juggle -- animated juggling patterns====================================    juggle -p 3    juggle -p 441    juggle -r 5It draws the balls in flight between two hands, numbered (1), (2), (3),thrown and caught in the pattern you name, over and over.  ^C ends it.A pattern is written in site-swap notation: each digit is how high a throwgoes, in beats, so `3' is the three-ball cascade, `51' a shower, `441' and`531' are three-ball tricks and `566151' needs four.  The number of ballsis the average of the digits; juggle refuses a pattern that cannot bejuggled.  `-r n' makes up a random pattern of n throws, `-s' sets the timestep between frames (0.2 by default; smaller is smoother), `-h' holds a2-throw in the hand, and `-n' gives the pattern a title.The full explanation, with the diagrams, is in SRC/juggle/ORIG/juggle.txt.By Glenn Hutchings, 1995, under the GNU General Public License(SRC/juggle/COPYING).